.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH REJMERGE 8 "" "imai #VERSION#" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
rejmerge \- review and merge files rejected during package upgrades
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rejmerge
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rejmerge
walks /var/lib/pkg/rejected under the selected root and compares rejected
incoming files with their installed counterparts. Root privileges are required.
Regular files offer keep, upgrade, merge, diff and skip choices. Other file
types offer keep, upgrade, diff and skip. Permission differences are presented
separately. Identical regular copies and rejected files without an existing
installed counterpart are removed automatically.
.PP
Keep discards the rejected file; upgrade replaces the installed entry with it;
skip leaves it for later. Merge produces a proposed result that can be viewed,
edited and explicitly installed. Review conflict markers before installation.
The default merge is a two-file merge, not a three-way merge using an ancestor.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " path
Select another system's root instead of /. Its rejected directory and
etc/rejmerge.conf are used.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the invoked command name, imai identity and version, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The C executable handles traversal, menu decisions and terminal input through
an embedded Bash compatibility controller. If present, /etc/rejmerge.conf under
the selected root is sourced in a persistent Bash context. It is trusted
executable code with root privileges and can redefine controller variables and
functions, including the lifecycle. File-operation, diff, merge and editor
callbacks execute in that context. In particular:
.TP
.B rejmerge_diff()
Called with installed path $1, rejected path $2 and output temporary path $3.
Write the difference to $3. The default uses diff -u.
.TP
.B rejmerge_merge()
Called with the same three arguments. Write the proposed merged result to $3.
The default uses GNU diff group-format options and inserts conflict markers.
The function may set REJMERGE_MERGE_INFO to text displayed after the merge.
.PP
Example override using explicitly quoted pathnames:
.PP
.nf
rejmerge_diff() {
        diff -u "$1" "$2" > "$3"
}
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Editor command, with optional whitespace-separated arguments, used on the
proposed merge. Default: vi. The value is split into command arguments, not
evaluated as shell syntax.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Requires Bash, mktemp, file, standard shell utilities, a pager named more
and the selected editor. GNU diffutils is required for the default merge
format. Metadata handling requires GNU or BusyBox stat with -L and -c support;
POSIX stat portability is not claimed. BusyBox alone is insufficient.
.SH SAFETY
This interactive command uses ordinary pathname-based operations, not the
C installer's root-scoped descriptor API. An alternate root is not a sandbox
or a security boundary against concurrent pathname replacement or symlink
changes. Only operate on trusted, controlled trees and trusted configuration.
Do not assume installer lookup guarantees apply to rejmerge.
.PP
Choices mutate files immediately; there is no transaction or rollback.
Interruption can leave earlier choices applied. Inspect installed ownership,
permissions and content after merging, especially for special files and links.
Compatibility with every configuration override and terminal environment
has not been established.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Normally zero after the interactive walk completes, nonzero for reported
argument, dependency, I/O or interruption failures. Configuration overrides
can change behavior and status. A completed walk does not imply that all
rejected files were accepted; skipped files remain for another run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/rejmerge.conf
Trusted executable configuration, relative to the selected root.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkg/rejected/
Rejected package entries, relative to the selected root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkgadd (8),
.BR pkgrm (8),
.BR pkginfo (8),
.BR pkgmk (8),
.BR diff (1),
.BR bash (1)
